The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (likely(!skb_cloned(skb) &&
           (!skb_is_nonlinear(skb) || !skb_has_frag_list(skb)))) {
    nsg = 1 + skb_shinfo(skb)->nr_frags;
} else {
    nsg = skb_cow_data(skb, 0, &unused);
}
```
Reason:
The original code checks if the socket buffer (skb) is cloned or nonlinear before deciding whether to call `skb_cow_data()`. If the skb is cloned or nonlinear, it calls `skb_cow_data()`, but if it is not, it directly accesses `skb_shinfo(skb)->nr_frags`. This can lead to modifying shared frags without proper handling, causing data corruption in other SKBs.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
nsg = skb_cow_data(skb, 0, &unused);
if (unlikely(nsg < 0)) {
    pr_err("RX: skb_cow_data() returned %d\n", nsg);
    return nsg;
}
```
Reason:
The patch simplifies the logic by always calling `skb_cow_data()` regardless of whether the skb is cloned or nonlinear. This ensures that the data is safely copied before any modifications are made, preventing unintended side effects on shared frags and thus fixing the bug.

